Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Late-onset asthma, Peripheral eosinophilia, Mononeuritis multiplex

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-013
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of right foot drop and severe, tearing abdominal pain in a patient with poorly controlled adult-onset asthma, requiring immediate medical evaluation to rule out acute mesenteric ischemia and progressive vasculitic neuropathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden asymmetric limb weakness combined with severe abdominal pain in a patient with a history of refractory asthma. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was critical to confirm mononeuritis multiplex, assess for acute surgical abdomen secondary to eosinophilic gastroenteritis or vasculitic ischemia, and facilitate immediate hospital transfer. Delaying until standard hours risked irreversible bowel necrosis or permanent paralysis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My asthma has been terrible, and a few hours ago I suddenly couldn't lift my right foot at all, and now my stomach is in absolute agony."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-year history of late-onset asthma that remains poorly controlled despite maximal inhaler therapy, accompanied by chronic allergic rhinitis and recurrent nasal polyps. Today, the patient experienced the acute, painless onset of right foot drop (asymmetrical peripheral neuropathy manifesting as mononeuritis multiplex). Within the last two hours, the patient developed severe, diffuse abdominal pain. Reports persistent low-grade fever, profound fatigue, and unintentional weight loss over the past month. Denies chest pain, but notes increasing dyspnea disproportionate to wheeze.
Functional Status: Unable to ambulate safely due to right foot drop and severe abdominal pain; confined to bed.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; roommate is highly anxious regarding the sudden deterioration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.7 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, writhing slightly in bed, tachypneic, and guarding the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Bilateral expiratory wheeze, though air entry is reasonable. Neurological: Profound 1/5 weakness in right ankle dorsiflexion and eversion (peroneal nerve palsy); left side intact. Sensation decreased over the lateral aspect of the right lower leg. Abdomen: Diffusely tender, heavily guarded globally, with sluggish bowel sounds, raising high suspicion for eosinophilic gastroenteritis or mesenteric vasculitis. Dermatological: Scattered subcutaneous nodules and palpable purpura noted on the distal lower extremities. Diagnostic tetrad completely aligns clinically: late-onset refractory asthma, mononeuritis multiplex, and high clinical prediction for striking peripheral eosinophilia and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA positivity pending emergent hospital laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Clear environment, but patient requires immediate extraction.
Medication Reconciliation: Using high-dose inhaled steroids and frequent salbutamol with poor effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute vasculitic crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Mesenteric Ischemia (Thrombotic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dominating presence of systemic vasculitic signs (mononeuritis multiplex, purpura, refractory asthma) which points toward an inflammatory microvascular occlusion or eosinophilic infiltration rather than a pure macrovascular atherosclerotic clot.

Severe Asthma Exacerbation with unrelated nerve compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The multi-system, acute deterioration (rash, acute severe abdominal pain, sudden nerve palsy) which cannot be explained by asthma alone and defines a systemic vasculitis.

Polyarteritis Nodosa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent, defining respiratory involvement (late-onset severe asthma, rhinosinusitis) which strongly favors EGPA.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute surgical abdomen and mobility failure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in an acute, multi-system vasculitic crisis, defined by the sudden onset of mononeuritis multiplex (foot drop) and severe abdominal pain, set against a background of refractory late-onset asthma. The acute abdominal presentation carries a high risk for ischemic bowel secondary to vasculitis or severe eosinophilic gastroenteritis. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to triage the acute abdomen, document the neurological deficit, and arrange immediate emergency transfer. Leaving this patient unassessed posed an immediate, life-threatening risk of bowel perforation and permanent neurological damage, fulfilling the criteria for urgent MBS 585-600 service provision.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is critically unwell and requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance. Immediate requirements upon arrival include urgent surgical assessment of the abdomen, abdominal CT, comprehensive blood work (full blood count for eosinophilia, ANCA panel), chest imaging, and initiation of high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids (and potentially cyclophosphamide) to halt the acute vasculitic ischemia.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) enforcement: Immediately ceasing all oral intake; directly explaining that because the bowel is under severe stress and surgery may be required, keeping the stomach empty is the most critical action to prevent complications during anesthesia. Immobilization: Remaining strictly flat in bed until paramedics arrive; avoiding the physical stress of trying to walk on a paralyzed foot will prevent an inevitable fall.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer organized (Call 000). If the patient loses consciousness, if the abdominal pain becomes abruptly sharp and agonizing, or if severe chest pain develops (risk of myocarditis), inform the paramedics immediately to upgrade the response.

PATIENT INFORMATION

